>So is 18/01 = 19 and 19 = 118 (18 + 100)? I always wondered about this. 
>Is there any reason for this "weird" numbering method? I mean if you're 
>gonna go above 18, why not call it 19, 20, 21, etc. like normal human 
>beings instead of 18/01, 18/02 etc. It just confuses people. I assume it 
>comes from D&D. Any "good" reasons for this? Historical?

It's from 1st and 2nd edition D&D, where they wanted a greater division in
strength between different creatures.

The normal maximum for humans in D&D was 18, 18/100 for strength. 19 or
above was reserved for superhuman level creatures, like giants, titans, and
gods. Certain magic items could of course allow players to reach those
strength levels.

The fractional 18 attribute was only available to fighter type classes.
Fighters, Paladins, Rangers, Barbarians, etc. If one of those types rolled
an 18, then they rolled percentile dice to see how much better it would go.
If say, a Cleric rolled an 18, then they would be stuck at 18.


